.TH EJSON-DECRYPT 1 "January 2026" "ejson" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ejson-decrypt \- decrypt secrets in EJSON, ETOML, or EYAML files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ejson decrypt
[\fB\-k\fR \fIkeydir\fR | \fB\-\-keydir\fR=\fIkeydir\fR]
[\fB\-o\fR \fIoutput\fR]
[\fB\-\-key\-from\-stdin\fR]
[\fB\-\-trim\-underscore\-prefix\fR]
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ejson decrypt
decrypts an encrypted secrets file and outputs the decrypted content. By
default, the decrypted content is written to stdout.
.PP
The decryption uses the private key corresponding to the public key embedded
in the file's
.B _public_key
field. The private key must be available either in the keydir (a file named
after the public key containing the private key) or provided via stdin using
the
.B \-\-key\-from\-stdin
option.
.PP
Encrypted values (those starting with
.BR "EJ[" )
are decrypted and replaced with their plaintext values. Unencrypted values
(including those under underscore-prefixed keys) are passed through unchanged.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keydir =\fIpath\fR
Directory containing private keys. Defaults to
.IR /opt/ejson/keys .
Can also be set via the
.B EJSON_KEYDIR
environment variable. The command-line option takes precedence.
.TP
.BI \-o " output"
Write the decrypted output to the specified file instead of stdout. The
output file is created with restrictive permissions (0600) to prevent
unauthorized access to the decrypted secrets.
.TP
.B \-\-key\-from\-stdin
Read the private key from standard input instead of looking it up in the
keydir. This is useful for environments where the private key is stored
in a secrets manager or passed via a secure channel. The key should be
provided as a 64-character hex-encoded string.
.TP
.B \-\-trim\-underscore\-prefix
Strip the first leading underscore from keys in the decrypted output. This
transforms keys like
.I _database_username
to
.IR database_username ,
while preserving the
.B _public_key
field. Keys with multiple leading underscores have only one underscore
removed (e.g.,
.I __key
becomes
.IR _key ).
.SH FILE FORMAT
Format detection is automatic based on file extension:
.TP
.B JSON
Extensions:
.IR .ejson ", " .json
.TP
.B TOML
Extensions:
.IR .etoml ", " .toml
.TP
.B YAML
Extensions:
.IR .eyaml ", " .eyml ", " .yaml ", " .yml
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EJSON_KEYDIR
Directory containing private keys. Defaults to
.IR /opt/ejson/keys .
Can be overridden by the
.B \-\-keydir
option.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Decrypt a file to stdout:
.RS
.nf
$ ejson decrypt secrets.ejson
{
  "_public_key": "63ccf05a9492e68e12eeb1c705888aebdcc0080af7e594fc402beb24cce9d14f",
  "_database_username": "admin",
  "database_password": "supersecret123"
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Decrypt to a file:
.RS
.nf
$ ejson decrypt -o decrypted.json secrets.ejson
$ cat decrypted.json
{
  "_public_key": "63ccf05a...",
  "database_password": "supersecret123"
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Decrypt with a custom keydir:
.RS
.nf
$ ejson decrypt -k /etc/myapp/keys secrets.ejson
.fi
.RE
.PP
Decrypt using a private key from stdin (useful with secrets managers):
.RS
.nf
$ vault read -field=private_key secret/ejson | ejson decrypt --key-from-stdin secrets.ejson
.fi
.RE
.PP
Decrypt and trim underscore prefixes from keys:
.RS
.nf
$ ejson decrypt --trim-underscore-prefix secrets.ejson
{
  "_public_key": "63ccf05a...",
  "database_username": "admin",
  "database_password": "supersecret123"
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Decrypt and pipe to another command:
.RS
.nf
$ ejson decrypt secrets.ejson | jq '.database_password'
"supersecret123"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Decrypt a YAML file:
.RS
.nf
$ ejson decrypt config.eyaml
_public_key: "63ccf05a..."
database_username: admin
database_password: supersecret123
.fi
.RE
.PP
Decrypt a TOML file:
.RS
.nf
$ ejson decrypt config.etoml
_public_key = "63ccf05a..."
database_username = "admin"
database_password = "supersecret123"
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. The file was decrypted successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (file not found, invalid format, missing private key,
decryption failed, etc.).
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
The scheme is selected automatically from the document's
.B _public_key.
Legacy
.B v1
documents use NaCl Box (Curve25519 + XSalsa20 + Poly1305); hybrid
.B v2
documents use X25519 + ML-KEM-768 with XChaCha20-Poly1305. See
.BR ejson (5).
.IP \(bu 2
When using
.BR \-o ,
the output file is created with restrictive permissions (0600) ensuring
only the owner can read and write the file.
.IP \(bu 2
The decryption process uses secure memory handling with automatic
zeroization of sensitive data (private keys) after use.
.IP \(bu 2
When writing to an output file, an atomic write pattern is used (write to
temporary file, then rename) to prevent race conditions and partial writes.
.IP \(bu 2
Maximum file size is limited to 10 MB to prevent denial of service through
memory exhaustion.
.IP \(bu 2
Avoid passing private keys via command-line arguments as they may be visible
in process listings. Use
.B \-\-key\-from\-stdin
instead for secure key handling.
.IP \(bu 2
Be cautious when decrypting to stdout in shared environments, as the output
may be captured in terminal scrollback or logs.
.IP \(bu 2
Path traversal attacks are prevented through validation (no device files,
FIFOs, or sockets are allowed).
.SH TIPS
.IP \(bu 2
Use
.B \-\-key\-from\-stdin
when integrating with secrets managers like HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets
Manager, or similar tools.
.IP \(bu 2
Decrypt to a file with
.B \-o
when you need the decrypted content to persist, ensuring proper file
permissions are automatically set.
.IP \(bu 2
Pipe the decrypted output to
.BR jq (1)
or
.BR yq (1)
for extracting specific values.
.IP \(bu 2
Use
.B \-\-trim\-underscore\-prefix
when consuming decrypted secrets in systems that don't expect
underscore-prefixed keys.
.IP \(bu 2
In shell scripts, use command substitution to avoid writing secrets to disk:
.RS
.nf
DB_PASS=$(ejson decrypt secrets.ejson | jq -r '.database_password')
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ejson (1),
.BR ejson (5),
.BR ejson\-encrypt (1),
.BR ejson\-keygen (1),
.BR ejson\-env (1)
